,title,link,text,language,family
0,Kazukuru language - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kazukuru_language,"Arthur Capell suggested that Kazukuru was a non-Austronesian (Papuan) language, and Stephen Wurm accordingly placed all three languages in a 'Kazukuru family' within the East Papuan phylum.",Kazukuru,Austronesian
1,ABVD: Kazukuru,https://abvd.eva.mpg.de/austronesian/language.php?id=560,"Updating Classification to Match Ethnologue 16. From: East Papuan, Yele-Solomons-New Britain, Yele-Solomons, Kazukuru To: Austronesian, Malayo-Polynesian, Central-Eastern, Eastern Malayo-Polynesian, Oceanic, Western Oceanic, Meso Melanesian, New Ireland, South New Ireland-Northwest Solomonic, New Georgia, West - Simon Greenhill (2009-07-23 03: ...",Kazukuru,Austronesian
2,Is Kazukuru really non-Austronesian? - The Australian ...(PDF) Is Kazukuru Really Non-Austronesian? - Academia.eduAustronesian Music - YouTubeABVD: Kazukuru(PDF) Is Kazukuru Really Non-Austronesian? - ResearchGateIs Kazukuru Really Non-Austronesian?,https://researchportalplus.anu.edu.au/en/publications/is-kazukuru-really-non-austronesian/,"Kazukuru has generally been considered to be a Papuan, that is, non-Austronesian, language, mostly on the basis of its lexicon. Reevaluation of the available data suggests a high likelihood that Kazukuru was in fact an Oceanic Austronesian language. Kazukuru has generally been considered to be a Papuan, that is, non-Austronesian, language, mostly on the basis of its lexicon. Reevaluation of the available data suggests a high likelihood that Kazukuru was in fact an Oceanic Austronesian language. Hi everyone! Welcome to Austronesian Music🎶This channel mainly posts music from Austronesian countries and languages, and also other songs of languages of Southeast Asia. Updating Classification to Match Ethnologue 16. From: East Papuan, Yele-Solomons-New Britain, Yele-Solomons, Kazukuru To: Austronesian, Malayo-Polynesian, Central-Eastern, Eastern Malayo-Polynesian, Oceanic, Western Oceanic, Meso Melanesian, New Ireland, South New Ireland-Northwest Solomonic, New Georgia, West - Simon Greenhill (2009-07-23 03: ... Jun 1, 2007 · The extent and possible causes of Kazukuru lexical deviations from the Austronesian norm are evaluated and discussed. Kazukuru has generally been considered to be a Papuan, that is, non-Austronesian, language, mostly on the basis of its lexicon. Reevaluation of the available data suggests a high likelihood that Kazukuru was in fact an Oceanic Austronesian language. Was Kazukuru an Oceanic Austronesian language? Reevaluation of the available data suggests a high likelihood that Kazukuru was in fact an Oceanic Austronesian language. Pronominal paradigms are clearly of Austronesian origin, and many other aspects of language structured retrievable from the limited data are also congruent with regional Oceanic Austronesian typology. Is Kazukuru a Papuan language? Is Kazukuru Really Non-Austronesian? Kazukuru is an extinct language, originally spoken in the inland of the western part of the island of New Georgia, Solomon Islands, and attested by very limited historical sources. Kazukuru has generally been considered to be a Papuan, that is, non-Austronesian, language, mostly on the basis of its lexicon. Is Kazukuru a real language? Kazukuru is an extinct language that was once spoken in New Georgia, Solomon Islands. The Dororo and Guliguli languages (if they even existed) were transcriptional variants, dialects, or closely related. The speakers of Kazukuru gradually merged with the Roviana people from the sixteenth century onward and adopted Roviana as their language. When was Kazukuru last recorded? Kazukuru was last recorded in the early twentieth century when its speakers were in the last stages of language shift. Today, Kazukuru is the name of a clan in the Roviana people group. Most of what is known about Kazukuru was collected by W.H.L. Waterhouse and published with S.H. Ray in an article in 1931.",Kazukuru,Austronesian
